<article class="env-exercise" id="059A">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/059A">Exercise <span data-tag="059A">4.7.9.4</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category. The collection of objects of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is equipped with an equivalence relation, where objects $X$ and $Y$ are considered to be equivalent if both $\operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(X,Y)$ and $\operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(Y,X)$ are nonempty. Let $Q$ denote the set of equivalence classes, and for each object $X \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ write $[X]$ for its equivalence class. Show that: </p>
<p><ul>
<li><p>
There is a partial ordering $\leq _{Q}$ on the set $Q$, where $[X] \leq _{Q} [Y]$ if and only if there exists a morphism from $X$ to $Y$ in the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
There is a unique functor $F: \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\rightarrow \operatorname{N}_{\bullet }(Q)$ which carries each object $X \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ to the isomorphism class $[X] \in Q$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
The functor $F$ exhibits $\operatorname{N}_{\bullet }(Q)$ as a homotopy $0$-category of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, in the sense of Definition <a data-tag="0597" href="/tag/0597">4.7.9.1</a>. 
</p>
</li>
</ul></p>
</article>
